artifact.self_contained_problem:
In constructive quantum field theory, a model is typically analyzed by a convergent multi-scale expansion (for instance a cluster or loop-vertex expansion) that controls the functional integral in a single renormalization-group regime, such as the small-coupling regime in which perturbation theory in the coupling constant g is convergent after Borel summation, or the large-N regime in which expansion in 1/N is convergent for fixed g. For models that exhibit genuinely non-perturbative phases (e.g. asymptotically free theories whose effective coupling becomes large in the infrared, theories with dynamical mass generation, or theories with phase transitions), no single such expansion is uniformly convergent across all scales.

Problem. Develop a rigorous mathematical framework for "gluing" together different convergent expansions associated with different renormalization-group regimes (for example a small-coupling expansion valid at high momentum scales and a 1/N expansion valid at low momentum scales, or vice versa) into a single multi-scale expansion that:

(a) is absolutely convergent uniformly in the ultraviolet and infrared cutoffs;
(b) produces well-defined Schwinger functions satisfying the Osterwalder–Schrader axioms (or a suitable Euclidean analogue) for the limiting model;
(c) gives non-perturbative control over correlation functions across the transition between regimes, in particular reproducing the expected non-perturbative phenomena (mass gap, symmetry breaking, or analogous features) of the model.

Concretely, construct such a glued expansion and use it to give a rigorous construction of at least one interacting four-dimensional Euclidean field theory whose existence requires crossing between a perturbative coupling regime and a 1/N (or other non-perturbative) regime under the renormalization group flow.

